            <ArticleTitle>Evaluation of static and dynamic postural balance in children with juvenile idiopathic arthritis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>150-7; discussion 157</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/PEP.0b013e31828a2978</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate balance in children with lower limb involvement due to juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Twenty-five children with JIA manifesting lower extremity arthritis within the previous year and 36 children who were healthy (aged 8-18 years) were assessed by using the Biodex Balance System (BBS) (Biodex, Shirley, New York). Single-leg static balance (BBS level 12) and bilateral static and dynamic balance (BBS levels 2 and 7) were measured. Lower extremity strength, disease activity, function, and pain were also assessed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">: Ten subjects with JIA (40%) could not complete single-leg balance testing, while all controls did (P &lt; .0001). Bilateral dynamic balance was impaired only at BBS level 2 (most unstable). Lower extremity weakness correlated with poor balance.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A significant proportion of children with leg arthritis have impaired balance. Proprioceptive exercises may emerge as an important therapy in the treatment of lower extremity arthritis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
